description:
src/memory.rs:617-654: BFS that for each frontier id, scans all store.nodes.values() looking for nodes whose parent_id matches. With N total nodes and a subtree of depth D, the total work is O(N * D).

Workspace integration tests have <10 nodes per account, so this never matters in tests. But the MemoryBackend is documented (src/memory.rs:5-13) as 'a documented, complete, source-readable implementation' and 'reference implementation' for contributors writing real backends. A reader studying this code will see O(N*D) and conclude the tree-traversal pattern is acceptable.

Fix is trivial: build a parent_id → children map once per call (or maintain it as an invariant on insert/destroy):

  let mut by_parent: HashMap<Option<&Id>, Vec<&FileNode>> = HashMap::new();
  for n in store.nodes.values() {
      by_parent.entry(n.parent_id.as_ref()).or_default().push(n);
  }
  // then BFS using by_parent.get(&Some(current)).

Time becomes O(N + descendants). Same complexity class as a real backend would use.

Reference-impl quality justifies the small extra code. Without the fix, contributors copying the MemoryBackend pattern will introduce N*D blowups into production backends.
